<h2>What I actually do</h2>

<p>
    I am the only QA engineer on this digital health platform, which reaches
    {% metric "monthly_users" %} in {% metric "languages" %} languages across
    {% metric "countries" %} countries. My job is the evidence layer underneath a claim nobody can
    make by inspection: that a generated answer is safe to send.
</p>

<p>
    That breaks down into three things. Golden datasets and judge design, which is how we decide
    what good looks like before a release. Production monitoring, which is how we find out we were
    wrong about it. And the test infrastructure underneath both: Playwright, API and contract
    suites, and the CI they run in.
</p>

<h2>How I got here</h2>

<p>
    Jeavio put me on Nivi in January 2022, as an intern, and never took me off it.
    {{ timeline.roles | length }} titles since, one platform, one client, {% metric "experience_years" %}.
    The most recent one changed nothing about the scope; the title just caught up with it. Depth
    over breadth, and I know which of those a hiring manager is usually told to prefer.
</p>

<p>
    The project had no QA process yet, so the first year was building one from zero: templates,
    severity standards, the first UI automation suite, the first API suite, the first CI
    integration. It is unglamorous work, and it is the reason I have opinions about test
    infrastructure that are not borrowed. That year alone: {% metric "defect_escape_rate_reduction" %},
    {% metric "associate_defects_caught" %}, and {% metric "etl_anomalies_surfaced" %}.
</p>

<p>
    AI testing started in September 2023 with intent classification and conversational flows. The
    real shift was Q1 2025, when I built the platform's first LLM-as-a-judge. I then found that
    it was grading answers produced by the same model family that generated them. Everything I have
    built since traces back to that, because the question it raised is the interesting one:
    <a href="{{ '/work/llm-judge-independence/' | url }}">what makes an evaluation independent
    enough to be worth anything?</a>
</p>

<p>
    Two things nobody asked me to do. I once went looking through production traffic with no bug
    report and no complaint behind it, just curious whether a routing decision was actually as
    efficient as it looked. {% metric "unnecessary_llm_invocations" %} were unnecessary, and nobody
    had measured that before I did.
    <a href="{{ '/writing/audit-your-own-traffic/' | url }}">The rest of it is here.</a> And the
    schema-driven test-data generator I maintain exists because I got tired of rewriting the same
    fixture code across suites, not because anyone asked for a public tool:
    {% metric "pypi_downloads_30d" %} in the trailing 30 days against {% metric "pypi_downloads" %}
    total says the interest outlasted the launch.
</p>

<h2>What I am like to work with</h2>

<p>
    A senior engineer on the team needed to start contributing to Jeavio's company-wide Robot
    Framework base, and had never written a test in code before. She was senior to me. There was
    no training path for that, so I built one and assigned her progressively harder work,
    reviewing it rather than leaving her to work it out alone. I ran a separate four-month
    programme for two campus recruits the same year. {% metric "engineers_upskilled" %} in total,
    and what stuck with me is that mentoring well means designing a path for each person's
    specific gap rather than running the same onboarding on everyone.
</p>

<p>
    I have been Scrum Master since April 2025, on top of QA and analytics,
    {% metric "scrum_master_sprints" %} of it by now. Earlier this year a ticket we needed sat
    unacknowledged for three days, blocking two of my engineers, because it depended on a firewall
    change only the platform team could make. Instead of adding another comment to the ticket, I
    called their Product Owner directly, Scrum Master to Product Owner, and we agreed on a fix in
    about fifteen minutes. Both engineers were unblocked by end of day. The failure wasn't the
    ticket sitting idle; it was mine, at planning, for pulling a cross-team dependency into a
    sprint and assuming it would resolve itself on its own schedule.
</p>

<p>
    I write down what went wrong. Every case study on this site has a section on what I would do
    differently, and at least one item in it is still open. This is not self-deprecation. It is
    the only way I know to make the rest of the claims checkable. If someone tells you their
    systems have no limitations, they have not measured them.
</p>

<p>
    I am careful about attribution. Two of the largest performance improvements in the pipeline I
    work on were other engineers' work; I built the measurement that attributed them. Where that
    is true on this site, it says so on the same line as the number, not in a footnote.
</p>

<p>
    I hedge in proportion to the evidence and no further. {% metric "golden_dataset_india_mh" %} is
    a count, so it is stated as a count. A self-tracked estimate says it is self-tracked. A replay
    projection says it never happened in production.
</p>

<h2>What I argue about</h2>

{#
  Inline rather than a data file, deliberately.

  The data layer stops a fact drifting between the pages that render it. These
  render here and nowhere else, so there is nothing to keep in sync. More to
  the point: most of these carry a link mid-sentence into the case study that
  already tells the story, and a JSON string cannot hold one without `| safe`,
  which nothing else in this repo does to author prose. A `{% for %}` would
  also render every entry to the same shape, which is the failure CONTENT.md
  names by hand — not every argument should open the same way.

  Move this to src/_data/opinions.json the day these need to render on a
  second route, and not before.
#}

<p>
    Positions I would defend if pushed on them, not consensus. A few of these are what testing is
    actually arguing about right now; the rest are older than that argument, and I think they get
    discarded too quickly under AI pressure.
</p>

<h3>Contested in 2026</h3>

<p>Where I land on the arguments still open.</p>

<div class="definition-list">
    <div class="definition-list__item">
        <h4 class="definition-list__term">A model cannot be the only judge of a model in its own family</h4>
        <p class="definition-list__description">
            The judge inherits the generator's own preferences and its blind spots, so it
            reliably finds the generator's output reasonable. Obvious failures still surface;
            the subtle ones, which are the ones worth catching, do not. I built one of these
            before I understood that, and
            <a href="{{ '/work/llm-judge-independence/' | url }}">what it took to notice</a> is
            the more useful half of that story.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="definition-list__item">
        <h4 class="definition-list__term">Do not mistake a green golden-dataset run for working in production</h4>
        <p class="definition-list__description">
            A curated dataset can only test the phrasings someone thought to write, and real
            users are broader than any curator anticipates. A danger-sign gap sat invisible
            through four independent layers of testing, mine included, for exactly this reason.
            <a href="{{ '/work/production-evaluation-pipeline/' | url }}">Production monitoring
            is what actually caught it.</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<h3>Older than this argument</h3>

<p>What I think holds regardless of which model generated the answer.</p>

<div class="definition-list">
    <div class="definition-list__item">
        <h4 class="definition-list__term">Precision and recall are not worth the same</h4>
        <p class="definition-list__description">
            A cache miss falls through safely to live generation. A false hit gets served to
            someone as an answer. I set the two failure modes to different bars, precision high
            and recall loose, because folding them into one accuracy number pretends they cost
            the same.
            <a href="{{ '/writing/asymmetric-thresholds/' | url }}">They don't.</a>
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="definition-list__item">
        <h4 class="definition-list__term">Exhaust the tuning before you call a limit structural</h4>
        <p class="definition-list__description">
            I used to treat a framework's limits as a design flaw to work around. Then I hit a
            parallelisation ceiling a fifth worker couldn't move, alongside three other walls
            that had nothing to do with tuning, and only then did retiring the suite I had built
            become the right call rather than the lazy one.
            <a href="{{ '/work/from-robot-to-playwright/' | url }}">The four walls are the
            actual argument.</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

